| 487 | the compiled checker checks a `comprises` clause at the closed trait's immediate subtypes only, so a type declared in another unit below a generic subtrait of a closed trait is accepted though no listed type is above it | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (checker) | `Specification/basic/traits.tex`, "Trait Declarations" | `ProjectFortress/tests/XXXComprisesGenericChildAcrossComponents.fss` | the comprises ways note; climb batch 7C rung Y | siblings: 489, 490, 551. `everyKnownSubtypeListed` (`TypeHierarchyChecker.scala:405-415` at c124c6b65) sees only extenders known when the closed trait's unit is checked, so `trait MyIntegral extends Integral[\MyIntegral\] end` in another component compiles. The sound check is at object declarations. The test is walk's half (row 551). Under the team's clause with its bound `[\I extends Integral[\I\]\]`, not live on `main`, the program is valid and coverage by the five integer types is refused: it would discharge P-60's proof assumption for the library only; open for programs' own closed traits (`perf-probes/anyintegral-comprises-where/REPORT.md` section 2). full text: history, row 487 |
| 489 | the compiled checker's `comprises` rule for a generic subtrait of a generic closed trait reads the clause in the subtrait's own type-variable names, so its verdict on an extender changes when the extender renames its type variable | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (checker) | `Specification/basic/traits.tex`, "Trait Declarations" | `ProjectFortress/compiler_tests/XXXComprisesGenericRenamed.test` | climb batch 7C rung Y's skeptic | siblings: 487, 490. The test's `trait Paired[\S\] extends { Filled[\S\], Marked[\S, ZZ32\] }`, under `Crate[\T\] comprises { Filled[\T\] }`, is refused ('immediate subtype Marked is not eligible'), and accepted spelled `Paired[\T\]`; walk runs both. Cause: `TypeHierarchyChecker.scala:333` (at c124c6b65) checks the extender against `subst_comprises` (`:327`), in the subtrait's variables, not substituting the extender's arguments to the subtrait. It also hides a false clause in row 414's gap. Repair: `explorations/compile-ladder/rung-comprises-checker/SKEPTIC.md`; it changes approved code (POSITIONS, "`AnyIntegral`'s closure and how the checker reads `comprises`."). full text: history, row 489 |
| 490 | the compiled checker's `comprises` rule finds the extenders of a generic subtrait by the simple text of its name, over the unit and every api in its environment, so the extenders of an unrelated trait of the same name count | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (checker) | `Specification/basic/traits.tex`, "Trait Declarations" | none | climb batch 7C rung Y's skeptic | siblings: 487, 489. The match is `st.getName.getText.equals(tt.getName.getText)` (`TypeHierarchyChecker.scala:410` at c124c6b65). Api `SkNameA` declares `trait G[\X\] extends Closed`, which nothing extends, over `Closed comprises { Listed }` (api `SkNameD`), and imports `SkNameB.{K}`, where `SkNameB` declares an unrelated `G[\X\]` and `K extends { Listed, G[\ZZ32\] }`: `K` counts as an extender of `SkNameA`'s `G`, so the checker accepts `SkNameA` and refuses it without the import. The same match can refuse a true clause. Fix: compare declarations, not names. Probes `explorations/compile-ladder/rung-comprises-checker/probes/skeptic/SkName*.fsi`, gone. full text: history, row 490 |
